\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let the $n$ columns $M_j\in\mathbb R^p$ be independent with law $N_p(\mu,\Sigma)$, where $\Sigma$ is positive definite with eigenvalues $\eta_1\ge\cdots\ge\eta_k>1$ and $\eta_{k+1}=\cdots=\eta_p=1$, with $k$ fixed as $p,n\to\infty$. Put $\bar M=n^{-1}\sum_j M_j$, $\widehat M_j=M_j-\bar M$, $S=n^{-1}\widehat M\widehat M^T$, and write $l_1\ge\cdots\ge l_p\ge0$ for the sample eigenvalues. Assume $p/n\to\gamma\in(0,\infty)$.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Determine the asymptotic distributions of the leading sample eigenvalues $l_1,l_2,\ldots$ for these real spiked populations, identifying their appropriate centerings, scalings and joint laws throughout the different spike regimes, including the transition at $\eta=1+\sqrt\gamma$. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/0712.0849}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Find the limiting eigenvalue statistics for high-dimensional real Gaussian covariance matrices with finitely many population spikes, including phase transitions.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 8000005 (AMR-079-0005). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Percy Deift, Some Open Problems in Random Matrix Theory and the Theory of Integrable Systems, arXiv:0712.0849 (2007), Problem 5, pp. 4--5. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/0712.0849}.
\end{description}
\label{end:8000005}

